The bug was in `type_arguments` in `repo/compiler-core/src/format.rs`, the single function that formats the argument lists of both function-type annotations (`fn(...) -> ...`) and tuple-type annotations (`#(...)`). It mapped each type argument straight through `self.type_ast(type_)` without ever popping the comments that preceded it. Those comments then leaked into the next `pop_comments` call (typically the trailing-comment pass), which is why they got dropped or repositioned after the element.

The fix mirrors the existing pattern already used for value arguments in `fn_arg`:

```rust
fn type_arguments<'a>(&mut self, arguments: &'a [TypeAst], location: &SrcSpan) -> Document<'a> {
    let arguments = arguments
        .iter()
        .map(|type_| {
            let comments = self.pop_comments(type_.location().start);
            let doc = self.type_ast(type_);
            commented(doc, comments)
        })
        .collect_vec();
    self.wrap_arguments(arguments, location.end)
}
```

For each argument it pops the comments occurring before that argument's start position and prepends them via `commented`, keeping each comment attached to its argument. Trailing comments continue to be handled by `wrap_arguments` (unchanged).

Verified against all six requirements via the CLI (comment before fn-type arg, comment before tuple element, trailing comment, nested fn types, multiple consecutive comments, and the comment-free no-regression case), and confirmed the output is idempotent. No files under `tests/` were modified.
